Null pointers
=============

A null pointer is `*T(0)` — the result of casting integer 0
to a pointer type. Two null pointers of the same type compare
equal; null compares unequal to any real address. `bool(ptr)`
returns `false` for null, `true` for any non-null pointer.

Default-initialized `*T` (declared without an initializer)
zero-inits to null per the variable-declaration rules.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features

null == null
------------

    #test
    test_null_eq_null() -> unit
        var p: *int = *int(0)
        var q: *int = *int(0)
        assert(p == q, "two null *int values should compare equal")

null != real address
--------------------

    #test
    test_null_neq_real() -> unit
        var x: int = 42
        var real: *int = &x
        var nullp: *int = *int(0)
        assert(real != nullp, "real address should not equal null")
        assert(nullp != real, "null should not equal real address")

Default-initialized pointer is null
-----------------------------------

    #test
    test_default_init_is_null() -> unit
        var p: *int
        var nullp: *int = *int(0)
        assert(p == nullp, "default-init *int should be null")

